IN PLAIN WORDS
JavaScript makes you declare a variable with a keyword. Use const for values that never change and let for values that will. Names use camelCase.

CODE
```javascript
const birthYear = 1995;
// birthYear = 2000;   // TypeError: Assignment to constant variable.

let volume = 5;
volume = 8;            // allowed

const currentYear = 2026;
let age = 36;
age = 39;
console.log(age);      // 39
```

KEY POINTS
- Python: age = 30 just creates the variable. JavaScript: const age = 30; or let age = 30;
- Use const by default; let only when the value must change (counters, scores, age over time).
- Python uses snake_case (birth_year); JavaScript uses camelCase (birthYear): first word lowercase, each next word capitalized.

IN PLAIN WORDS
The same if / elif / else logic as Python with new syntax: elif becomes else if, conditions go in ( ), blocks go in { } instead of indentation, and and/or become && and ||.

CODE
```text
Python        | JavaScript
--------------|--------------
elif          | else if
indentation   | curly braces { }
if x > 5:     | if (x > 5) {
and           | &&
or            | ||
```

```javascript
let score = 65;
let hasBonus = true;
if (score >= 90) {
  console.log("S Rank");
} else if (score >= 75 && hasBonus) {
  console.log("A Rank");
} else if (score >= 60 || hasBonus) {
  console.log("B Rank");
} else {
  console.log("C Rank");
}
// B Rank
```

